                <pb facs="0525" n="413" sameAs="frus:frus1907p1/pg413"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 3294/7.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador McCormick
                        to the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1907-01-21"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">January 21,
                    1907</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. McCormick informs Mr. Root, in re Jacobs, that he is still in prison,
                    notwithstanding the repeated remonstrances against his long detention without
                    showing the cause. He also states that correspondence with the foreign office
                    shows evident intention of giving him up without even advising or consulting the
                    embassy, as is usually done. No extradition treaty was exchanged with the
                    Argentine Republic, but a reciprocity letter. Mr. McCormick further states that
                    he does not venture to push the matter further without instructions as Mr.
                    Jacobs’s position is peculiar with regard to his right to claim the protection
                    of the embassy, as shown by dispatch No. 204.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
